377
00:23:26,836 --> 00:23:27,917
[SPEAKER_01]: You get to see the knowledge.

378
00:23:28,418 --> 00:23:30,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, mindfulness results in knowledge.

379
00:23:30,360 --> 00:23:31,921
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a recognition of your situation.

380
00:23:31,981 --> 00:23:32,902
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what mindfulness is.

381
00:23:34,187 --> 00:23:35,467
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have too much of it, as I said.

382
00:23:35,927 --> 00:23:39,048
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, if you stick to it, knowledge of your situation will have to arise.

383
00:23:39,248 --> 00:23:41,069
[SPEAKER_00]: The certain, the characteristic, the obviousness.

384
00:23:41,689 --> 00:23:43,449
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature becomes apparent.

385
00:23:44,109 --> 00:23:47,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, I have concocted the nature through my theoretical reasoning.

386
00:23:48,190 --> 00:23:48,311
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

387
00:23:48,671 --> 00:23:57,653
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature will become apparent, known, discerned, evident, because you stop covering it up through mindfulness.

388
00:24:01,152 --> 00:24:14,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will stop covering it up, if you stop expecting, or if you stop basically feeling entitled, internally, to avoiding discomfort, to avoiding anxiety.

389
00:24:17,239 --> 00:24:24,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I don't say you need to seek it out like a maniac, but you certainly must not have the attitude of, this must be avoided at all cost.

390
00:24:25,904 --> 00:24:28,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, practice of dhamma must be avoided at all costs.

391
00:24:28,266 --> 00:24:28,946
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you're saying.

392
00:24:29,887 --> 00:24:30,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Inadvertently.

393
00:24:31,008 --> 00:24:35,051
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you're saying.

394
00:24:38,414 --> 00:24:42,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being concerned about your feeling, feelings.

395
00:24:42,417 --> 00:24:42,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

396
00:24:42,697 --> 00:24:47,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Being concerned, for example, about the fact that you're subjected to feeling, good or bad.

397
00:24:48,361 --> 00:24:49,622
[SPEAKER_01]: And that it will have to change.

398
00:24:49,662 --> 00:24:53,045
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, the change is implicit in a good feeling, the change is implicit in a bad feeling.

399
00:24:53,981 --> 00:24:55,142
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not wanting that.

400
00:24:55,162 --> 00:24:58,345
[SPEAKER_00]: You're wanting good feeling on your own terms and you're wanting bad feeling on your own terms.

401
00:24:58,465 --> 00:25:01,088
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, wanting to go away, wanting to come here.

402
00:25:02,149 --> 00:25:05,492
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you see that it's already... change is implicit, you wouldn't... you would stop resisting it.

403
00:25:06,252 --> 00:25:08,174
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's literally the Sotāpanna's insight.

404
00:25:09,275 --> 00:25:11,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, change is implicit in this thing that's here.

405
00:25:12,558 --> 00:25:12,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

406
00:25:13,719 --> 00:25:14,280
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you wouldn't be...

407
00:25:14,520 --> 00:25:16,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, coming and going is implicit in that change.

408
00:25:16,462 --> 00:25:17,403
[SPEAKER_01]: ...in that pleasure.

409
00:25:17,683 --> 00:25:19,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, being mindful of the pleasure means

410
00:25:21,569 --> 00:25:23,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Being mindful of the pleasure is not necessarily pleasant.

411
00:25:24,431 --> 00:25:27,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Being mindful of the nature of the pleasure can be very disconcerting.

412
00:25:28,433 --> 00:25:37,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for example, being mindful of the pleasure of sensuality is actually, oh my goodness, it's like being mindful of holding a deadly snake that you're not in control of.

413
00:25:38,500 --> 00:25:43,163
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you forget the deadliness of it, then you just... or the other one, the better simile, the poisonous drink.

414
00:25:43,995 --> 00:25:49,142
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're mindful of the poison in this beautifully smelling, tasting consistency of this drink...

415
00:25:49,163 --> 00:25:51,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, poison is undetectable, but you know it's in there.

416
00:25:53,329 --> 00:25:58,436
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're mindful of the poison of that drink, you'll be careful to not even spill it on your skin, let alone drink it.

417
00:25:58,957 --> 00:25:59,417
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you...

418
00:26:00,367 --> 00:26:03,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, forget that it's poisonous, or you'll be chugging it down.

419
00:26:04,389 --> 00:26:05,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, yeah, you can't have...

420
00:26:06,270 --> 00:26:08,071
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I know where the poison is.

421
00:26:08,531 --> 00:26:09,271
[SPEAKER_01]: And enjoy with it.

422
00:26:09,411 --> 00:26:14,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me enjoy the pleasant part of it, because it will make the poison disappear.

423
00:26:14,274 --> 00:26:22,838
[SPEAKER_00]: That's you, basically, trying to, kind of, like, practicing mindfulness on your own terms, which is not mindfulness, and then trying to claim that, no, no, no, it's still on the right term.

424
00:26:22,878 --> 00:26:23,238
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still...

425
00:26:23,258 --> 00:26:24,418
[SPEAKER_00]: I still am aware of concern.

426
00:26:24,838 --> 00:26:27,660
[SPEAKER_00]: And monks did that in the suttas, when they said, in the Thimble of Sensuality,

427
00:26:29,080 --> 00:26:34,705
[SPEAKER_00]: When, basically, they thought that you could be mindful of central danger and engage with centrality at the same time.

428
00:26:35,086 --> 00:26:36,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said that's impossible.

429
00:26:36,948 --> 00:26:48,380
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's impossible to have central perceptions of, like, joyous, delightful, and, at the same time, be mindful of, basically, have central perceptions, not have central perceptions, as in, see this is dangerous,

430
00:26:49,935 --> 00:26:51,236
[SPEAKER_00]: And engage with centrality.

431
00:26:51,496 --> 00:26:54,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're engaging with centrality, it means perceptions of centrality has to be there.

432
00:26:55,198 --> 00:27:00,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning, if you're engaging with centrality, not seeing the danger of centrality has to be there.

433
00:27:00,561 --> 00:27:01,622
[SPEAKER_00]: It's mutually exclusive.

434
00:27:02,622 --> 00:27:05,584
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a person claims both, that's a contradiction in terms.

435
00:27:05,664 --> 00:27:08,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, ah, they're not seeing where real danger is.

436
00:27:08,786 --> 00:27:09,787
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

437
00:27:09,807 --> 00:27:15,250
[SPEAKER_00]: They take mindfulness on their own terms, and then they take the concern behind the mindfulness on their own terms.

438
00:27:15,590 --> 00:27:18,792
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they say, oh, no, it is concern, but it's not because it's on your own terms.

439
00:27:20,324 --> 00:27:22,006
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you took the ownership of it.

440
00:27:22,026 --> 00:27:23,188
[SPEAKER_00]: The concern is exactly that.

441
00:27:23,348 --> 00:27:27,053
[SPEAKER_00]: That which you cannot own, that you are subjected to, not other way around.

442
00:27:31,178 --> 00:27:34,119
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be drinking the poison if you knew it was poisonous.

443
00:27:34,499 --> 00:27:43,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you think you know it's poisonous, yet you're still drinking it, means you just took to think to know what poison is.

444
00:27:44,541 --> 00:27:49,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it were truly that poison that you are thinking of, you wouldn't even touch it.

445
00:27:49,642 --> 00:27:52,362
[SPEAKER_00]: You would... as the Buddha said, the danger would have become apparent.

446
00:27:52,722 --> 00:27:55,843
[SPEAKER_00]: And that would reveal itself to you, if you're self-honest.

447
00:27:56,423 --> 00:28:00,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have the faith in the Buddha's instruction, yet here you are contradicting that.

448
00:28:01,243 --> 00:28:07,107
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were honest, you would see that contradiction, and then you would take that as a symptom of, I'm not seeing this deeply enough.

449
00:28:07,708 --> 00:28:09,809
[SPEAKER_00]: It's exactly what I just said a few minutes ago.

450
00:28:10,670 --> 00:28:18,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I'm sure many people would think, oh, but I know what dukkha is, but I don't feel, like, extreme sense of urgency to practice dhamma, and I'm neither going mad.

451
00:28:19,656 --> 00:28:20,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you don't know what dukkha is.

452
00:28:22,823 --> 00:28:23,403
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha said.

453
00:28:23,423 --> 00:28:28,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you were to know what dukkha is, you would be full-on bent on practicing the dhamma, or losing your mind.

454
00:28:29,345 --> 00:28:30,705
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot bear that in itself.

455
00:28:31,445 --> 00:28:33,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Without addressing it, you cannot stay with it.

456
00:28:34,846 --> 00:28:38,067
[SPEAKER_01]: With dukkha in a purest form, that needs to be seen and understood.

457
00:28:39,667 --> 00:28:40,567
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha said, literally.

458
00:28:41,187 --> 00:28:44,468
[SPEAKER_00]: All he teaches is suffering and freedom from it.

459
00:28:44,608 --> 00:28:44,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

460
00:28:45,128 --> 00:28:46,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

461
00:28:46,329 --> 00:28:47,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything he ever said.

462
00:28:49,409 --> 00:28:51,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Not hard to find a way in between a bit of both.

463
00:28:51,910 --> 00:28:52,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

464
00:28:53,112 --> 00:28:59,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering... which... it's fine if you don't want to do it, but it's not fine if you claim you're doing it, while you're not doing it.

465
00:29:01,398 --> 00:29:04,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you did understand what dukkha is, you wouldn't...

466
00:29:05,161 --> 00:29:07,103
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be... the directions would be automatic.

467
00:29:07,863 --> 00:29:08,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

468
00:29:09,244 --> 00:29:16,270
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to deal with this, and if you're unable to deal with this, or if you try ignoring the fact that you need to deal with this, well, the other direction then is taken off madness.

469
00:29:17,130 --> 00:29:18,812
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be able to stay on that level.

470
00:29:21,273 --> 00:29:21,734
[SPEAKER_00]: So, either...

471
00:29:22,890 --> 00:29:27,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Dealing with it, or going mad, or maintain the cover-up, as in never really admit what's underneath.

472
00:29:27,692 --> 00:29:30,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you do admit what's underneath your cover-up, you cannot forget that.

473
00:29:32,234 --> 00:29:36,996
[SPEAKER_00]: And people who experience anxiety know that.

474
00:29:40,117 --> 00:29:41,357
[SPEAKER_01]: It's back to the poison drink.

475
00:29:41,457 --> 00:29:43,958
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... you wouldn't be...

476
00:29:44,739 --> 00:29:47,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Your actions reveal that you... you are drinking.

477
00:29:48,855 --> 00:29:51,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're concerned about yourself already.

478
00:29:51,617 --> 00:29:58,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you knew that this action was going to hurt you, even worse, you wouldn't be doing it.

479
00:29:58,602 --> 00:29:59,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a contradiction.

480
00:29:59,822 --> 00:30:08,008
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't say, yeah, poison, which can kill... That's what it would have meant, like, when he would say, like, you know, a man wishing for his welfare, not for his harm, would heed my instruction.

481
00:30:08,448 --> 00:30:13,972
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, even the one who doesn't accept his instruction, if he was self-honest, he would realize that it's for his welfare.

482
00:30:14,991 --> 00:30:22,553
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even the person who engages with sensuality and unwholesome behavior, deep down, it's always rooted in wanting their own welfare and not their own harm.

483
00:30:23,013 --> 00:30:26,334
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the universal principle for every human being, enlightened or unenlightened.

484
00:30:26,354 --> 00:30:28,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Engaging in sensuality, because you think it's good for you.

485
00:30:29,175 --> 00:30:29,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

486
00:30:29,535 --> 00:30:35,097
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you realize that it's... you can't both realize that it's bad for you... And engage with it.

487
00:30:35,217 --> 00:30:35,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

488
00:30:35,777 --> 00:30:35,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

489
00:30:36,077 --> 00:30:38,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the reason for engagement is to not see that it's bad.

